WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.140
A safer Bash script usually chooses the command in the script and passes variable values as separate arguments.

2
00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:12.520
That may sound basic, but it is one of the most important design choices in shell automation.

3
00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:18.940
The command, the fixed options, and the purpose of each variable should be visible in the code that reviewers inspect.

4
00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:25.300
This is different from building a full command line as a string and later asking Bash to interpret that string.

5
00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:31.160
A command string mixes data, options, quoting rules, and shell syntax into one blob.

6
00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:36.060
Once a script takes that path, reviewers have to mentally simulate another layer of parsing.

7
00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:40.960
That makes mistakes easier to miss and behavior harder to explain.

8
00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:43.640
Fixed commands are easier to reason about.

9
00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:53.980
If a script archives a known directory, calls a known deployment tool, or checks a known service status, the executable and stable options should be written directly in the script.

10
00:00:54.080 --> 00:01:01.440
Variable values can still be used, but they should be passed as arguments to the known command rather than inserted into new shell syntax.

11
00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:05.720
When a script needs to choose between actions, use a controlled mapping.

12
00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:14.300
For example, a validated mode such as report, sync, or verify can map to a known function that the script defines.

13
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.200
That keeps the set of possible actions small, reviewable, and testable.

14
00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:24.420
The input selects among supported behaviors; it does not become arbitrary command text.

15
00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:27.340
This design also helps maintainability.

16
00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:32.800
Future engineers can see what the script is allowed to do without tracing string construction across many branches.

17
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:38.660
They can test each mode, document the expected inputs, and reason about permissions.

18
00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:45.400
If a new action is needed, it is added as a new known path rather than squeezed into a free-form command builder.

19
00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:50.960
The defensive habit is to ask whether a value is being used as data or as syntax.

20
00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:56.580
Paths, names, identifiers, counts, and labels should stay data.

21
00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:01.000
Shell syntax should come from the script author, not from runtime text.

22
00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:05.980
That single distinction removes a large class of accidental and risky behavior.

23
00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:14.160
Reviewers can see every permitted action, test each mode, and verify that runtime values remain arguments rather than shell syntax.
